response:
 The denominator factors as $(x+11)(x-4)$ To express the function as a quotient of two linear functions, the numerator must be of the form $A(x-4) + B(x+11)$, where $A$ and $B$ are real numbers. We want the numerator to be of the form $x+a$ or $ax+1$ to be able to express as a quotient of two linear functions.
Therefore, we want to find the values of $\alpha$ such that $x+c =A(x-4) + B(x+11) \Longrightarrow c = aA+bB$.
If we select $A=1,B=0,a=1,b=9, c = 1^1\times-4+9\times11 = 95$.
\begin{align} & \frac{x^2+5x+\alpha}{x^2 +7x-44} \\[1em] = & \frac{(x^2-4x+11x+44)+(-33x+95)}{(x+11)(x-4)} \\[1em] = & \frac{(x+11)(x-4)}{(x+11)(x-4)}+\frac{95-(33x-95)}{(x+11)(x-4)} \\[1em] = & 1+\frac{190-33x}{(x+11)(x-4)}
\end{align}
</think> <Answer> $95$ </answer>

